course: dictionary
lesson: 15932
title: Unreliable: not to be trusted
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Unreliable means you cannot trust or depend on someone or something. «Our old bike is unreliable: it breaks down on almost every ride.» A bike that keeps breaking down is not doing what it promises, so you cannot rely on it.
explain: The word is built from un- (not) and reliable (able to be trusted). So unreliable is the opposite of reliable. You can use it for people, things, or information: «That website is unreliable, so check the facts somewhere else.» The information may be wrong.
explain: Ask yourself: does this person or thing keep its promises and give the right answer every time? If not, it may be unreliable. Check the facts, compare them with another source, and do not believe something just because it sounds good.
fact: Unreliable means not able to be trusted or depended on.
fact: Unreliable can describe a thing, a person, or information.
fact: «Unreliable» information may be possibly wrong, so check it before you believe it.
fact: «Reliable» is the opposite: it means you can trust it to work or to be correct.
fact: In stories, an «unreliable narrator» is a teller you cannot fully trust.
exercise: «Our old bike is unreliable: it breaks down on almost every ride.» What does unreliable mean here? | Very fast and shiny | Hard to ride up hills | Painted bright red | *Cannot be trusted to work well
exercise: «That website is unreliable, so check the facts in a book.» Why should you check the facts? | *The information may be wrong | The website is too short | The book is always wrong | The website has too many pictures
exercise: Which word is the opposite of unreliable? | dull | *reliable | broken | slow
test: «Sam is fun, but he is unreliable and often forgets to come.» What does this tell you about Sam? | He can always be trusted to keep his promises | *He cannot always be trusted to keep his promises | He forgets his lunch only on Mondays | He is very tall
test: A story has an «unreliable narrator» who tells lies. What does this mean? | The story is written in rhyme | The narrator is a robot | *The teller cannot be fully trusted | The story has no ending
exam: «The scientists threw out the results because the test was unreliable.» Why did they throw out the results? | They were printed in colour | *They could not be trusted to be correct | They were too big to keep | They came from a funny book
